---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Cost of Moving to Canada from Nigeria & Africa | AfriMigrate"
  description="A realistic, free breakdown of what it costs to move to Canada from Africa — government fees, settlement funds, tests, and flights. No consultant fees included."
>
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/canada">Canada</a> / Cost of Moving</div>
  <div class="eyebrow">Canada · Budgeting</div>
  <h1>Cost of Moving to Canada</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
    Consultants often quote thousands of dollars just to "help" with an application you can do yourself for free. Here's a realistic breakdown of the costs Canada's immigration system actually requires — figures are indicative in CAD and change over time, so always confirm current fees on the official IRCC fee schedule.
  </p>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Government Fees</div>
    <h2>What IRCC charges</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical cost (CAD)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Principal applicant processing fee</td><td>~$950</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spouse/partner processing fee</td><td>~$950</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dependent child processing fee (each)</td><td>~$260</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Right of Permanent Residence Fee (per adult)</td><td>~$575</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Biometrics fee (per person/family cap)</td><td>~$85 / ~$170</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Pre-Application Costs</div>
    <h2>Before you even apply</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Language test (IELTS/CELPIP)</td><td>$220–$320</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Educational Credential Assessment</td><td>$200–$300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Police clearance certificate(s)</td><td>Varies by country, often $20–$100 each</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medical exam (panel physician)</td><td>$150–$250 per person</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Settlement Funds</div>
    <h2>Proof of funds you'll need to show</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px;">
      Unless you're exempt (e.g. you already have valid, arranged Canadian employment), Express Entry applicants must show liquid settlement funds. These are not fees you pay — they're savings you need to demonstrate you have access to.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Family size</th><th>Approx. funds required (CAD)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1 person</td><td>~$14,700</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 people</td><td>~$18,300</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 people</td><td>~$22,500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 people</td><td>~$27,300</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px; margin-top: 1rem;">
      These figures are updated annually by IRCC — confirm the current amount before you apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="eyebrow">Getting There</div>
    <h2>Flights and initial settlement</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width: 700px;">
      Budget separately for one-way flights (typically $700–$1,500 per adult depending on origin city and season), initial accommodation deposits, and 1–2 months of living costs while you find your feet and open a bank account.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="manifesto">
      <h2>What you save by <em>not</em> hiring a consultant</h2>
      <p>Immigration consultants commonly charge $2,000–$10,000+ to prepare a single Express Entry application. AfriMigrate's calculator, checklist and guides do the same groundwork for free.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
